WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:09.604
A voice agent that sounds confident and is wrong is a liability.

2
00:00:09.604 --> 00:00:18.839
This is HarborVoice AI: a fictional port terminal, with an ElevenLabs voice agent called Nora, who answers shipment questions in four languages.

3
00:00:18.839 --> 00:00:21.340
Every record you will see is synthetic.

4
00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:25.142
Nora can do exactly two things.

5
00:00:25.142 --> 00:00:30.891
She can look up a shipment by its exact reference, and she can ask a person to review it.

6
00:00:30.891 --> 00:00:31.666
That is all.

7
00:00:31.666 --> 00:00:37.286
There is no tool to release cargo, so no wording from a caller can make her release it.

8
00:00:37.286 --> 00:00:38.513
The model proposes.

9
00:00:38.513 --> 00:00:40.580
The server decides what is true.

10
00:00:42.380 --> 00:00:46.503
Those rules live in code you can test without a model.

11
00:00:46.503 --> 00:00:48.488
No reference: ask for one.

12
00:00:48.488 --> 00:00:52.000
Unknown reference: say so, and invent nothing.

13
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.276
Lookup system down: say it is down, and confirm nothing.

14
00:00:56.276 --> 00:00:59.864
A record that looks wrong: send it to a person.

15
00:00:59.864 --> 00:01:00.780
Eight tests.

16
00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:01.620
No API key.

17
00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:02.460
No credits.

18
00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:08.065
Asking for a human is where many agents quietly mislead.

19
00:01:08.065 --> 00:01:11.734
Here, an escalation is a ticket marked pending review.

20
00:01:11.734 --> 00:01:16.557
Only an authenticated operator can approve or reject it, and only once.

21
00:01:16.557 --> 00:01:19.411
And approval does not change the shipment.

22
00:01:19.411 --> 00:01:22.740
The agent is told to say pending, never approved.

23
00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:28.513
To check behaviour, write down what should happen before you look.

24
00:01:28.513 --> 00:01:35.495
AgentProof shows a deliberately unsafe policy failing thirty of a hundred and ten scenarios, and exactly which ones.

25
00:01:35.495 --> 00:01:39.167
Here, the lookup failed and the answer still claimed success.

26
00:01:39.167 --> 00:01:43.260
Switch to the corrected policy, and the same case recovers honestly.

27
00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:48.036
AgentOps Kit does the same for configuration.

28
00:01:48.036 --> 00:01:53.392
Turn authentication off, or add a plain HTTP tool, and the check fails the build.

29
00:01:53.392 --> 00:01:56.500
Secrets are removed before anything is printed.

30
00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:00.272
Be clear about what this shows.

31
00:02:00.272 --> 00:02:02.053
These are scripted fixtures.

32
00:02:02.053 --> 00:02:03.770
They prove the checks work.

33
00:02:03.770 --> 00:02:09.240
They do not prove that a language model is reliable, and they say nothing about audio.

34
00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:13.820
The few real provider tests are reported separately, and they are small.

35
00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:18.267
The creative work is documented the same way.

36
00:02:18.267 --> 00:02:24.795
A narrated product film was rebuilt in ElevenCreative Flows after the first version lost the product on screen.

37
00:02:24.795 --> 00:02:30.500
The walkthrough shows the failure, the fix and the measurements, including what still goes wrong.

38
00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:33.942
Everything is open source.

39
00:02:33.942 --> 00:02:38.300
Two tutorials and a ninety-minute workshop are free, and run offline.

40
00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:42.721
HarborVoice is an independent project, not affiliated with ElevenLabs.

41
00:02:42.721 --> 00:02:45.500
Try it, break it, and tell us what you find.
